    "# Research Question"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "What demographic, legal, and geographic factors are associated with death row outcomes (execution, commutation, exoneration, or death from other causes) among individuals sentenced to death in the United States since 1976?\n",
    "-  Predictive variables of interest include: race, gender, age at sentencing, state, county, and sentencing year.\n",
    "- Factors that will be controlled for are: sentencing year and state.\n",
    "- Appeal-level information is not available in the dataset."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Background and Prior Work"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Our interest in research is on what factors are associated with death row outcomes among individuals sentenced to death in the United States since 1976, including demographics, legal factors, and geographic distribution. The death penalty in the United States remains one of the most researched topics of the criminal justice system, with outcomes on death row shaped by more than just legal facts. Since its reestablishment in 1976, thousands of individuals have been sentenced to death, executed, exonerated, had sentences commuted, or died from other causes while on death row<sup><a name=\"ref3\"></a>3</sup>. National data from *Death Row U.S.A.* indicate that death row populations are disproportionately male (98.9 %) and primarily White or Black, with significant variation across states, suggesting the importance of geographic context in analyzing death row outcomes<sup><a name=\"ref3\"></a>3</sup>.\n",
    "\n",
    "Research on specific jurisdictions has studied how demographic factors, particularly race and ethnicity, influence outcomes after a death sentence. Petrie and Coverdill researched Texas death row cases from 1974 to 2009 and found that the race of both offenders and victims affected the likelihood of execution, while having less impact on sentence relief such as commutation or overturning<sup><a name=\"ref5\"></a>5</sup>. These findings suggest that while executions may reflect demographic disparities, other outcomes are shaped by different factors, including appeals processes and legal context.\n",
    "\n",
    "The results in studies of high-activity death penalty counties show how race interacts with legal processes. Scott Phillips’ analysis of Harris County, Texas, shows that both defendant and victim race influenced whether a defendant was charged with capital murder and ultimately given the death penalty<sup><a name=\"ref4\"></a>4</sup>. Combined with national data, these studies indicate that death row outcomes arise from the intersection of individual characteristics, legal decision-making, and geographic variation. Understanding these factors is important when addressing questions about individual justice and disparities in the administration of capital punishment.\n",
    "\n",
    "1. <a name=\"cite_note-1\"></a> [^](#cite_ref-1) Furman v. Georgia. *Oyez*. https://www.oyez.org/cases/1971/69-5030\n",
    "2. <a name=\"cite_note-2\"></a> [^](#cite_ref-2) Gregg v. Georgia. *Oyez* https://www.oyez.org/cases/1975/74-6257\n",
    "3. <a name=\"cite_note3\"></a> [^](#cite_ref-3)Death Row U.S.A., Spring 2022 (As of April 1, 2022), Legal Defense Fund. https://heinonline.org/HOL/P?h=hein.journals/dprep8&i=1091\n",
    "4. <a name=\"cite_note-4\"></a> [^](#cite_ref-4)Michelle A. Petrie & James E. Coverdill, *Who Lives and Dies on Death Row? Race, Ethnicity, and Post‑Sentence Outcomes in Texas*. Social Problems, 57(4), 630–652. https://doi.org/10.1525/sp.2010.57.4.630"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Hypothesis\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We hypothesize that death row outcomes differ based on demographic and sentence-related factors, such as the individuals' year sentenced, the U.S. state in which the were sentenced, and race. We specifically believe that some states and ethnic groups will have higher rates of execution or exoneration than others, with the rate of total executions decreasing over time. This hypothesis is based on prior research that shows long-standing racial and geographical disparities in how the death penalty is applied."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Data"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Data overview\n",
    "\n",
    "1. An ideal dataset would include the following: \n",
    "   1. Demographic variables: race gender, age at time of sentencing, education level, socioeconomic indicators\n",
    "      Case information: state, county, offense type, legal history, year of sentence\n",
    "      Sentence outcomes: current status (executed, exonerated, resentenced, commuted, etc.)\n",
    "      Legal process details: appeals filed, number of trials, presence of public defenders\n",
    "      Contextual variables: victim characteristics, country crime rates, quality of legal representation\n",
    "   2. Since the data covers all individuals sentenced to death since 1976 in the U.S., a large sample size is preferable. This could be between 5,000-10,000.\n",
    "   3. Data would be collected from public government records, such as state/federal departments of corrections and court case records.\n",
    "   4. Data would be stored in a structured table, with one row per individual record and columns for each variable.\n",
    "2. Real datasets\n",
    "   1. Data located in a public GitHub repository: https://github.com/theintercept/the-condemned-data/blob/master/the-condemed-data.csv. It includes \"a comprehensive dataset on everyone sentenced to die in active death penalty jurisdictions since 1976\" and has 7,335 observations. Data was collected from state and federal departments of correction, state and federal public defenders, lawyers working for advocacy groups, and individuals who had been tracking this data in their respective states. Each row represents a death sentence with variables such as race, gender, state/county of prosecution, year sentenced, etc.\n",
    "   2. Data located on Death Penalty Information Center website, which is a national non-profit organization: https://deathpenaltyinfo.org/facts-and-research/data/executions. It includes data on 1655 executions from 1977-2026. In addition to the previous dataset, this includes Number, Race, and Sex of Victims, Method, Juvenile, Federal, Volunteer, and Foreign National as variables. \n"
   ]
  },
